## Forward source of each sensitivity block

| Current main equations | Forward source | Role in the full path |
|---|---|---|
| 14 | Timestep composition of 2–12 | Carries prior state response and adds direct control response. |
| 15–16 | Leg sharing 6; lane sharing 7 uses equal weights | Green and receiving changes become granted-rate changes. |
| 17 | Service-amount sum 9 | Combines rate and duration changes into transferred mass. |
| 18 | Head-clearance branch of 8 | Earlier/later head removal changes followers' service time. Other stopping branches are explicit in the companion. |
| 19 | Grid/overlap definition 12 and remaining ledger mass | Changed traffic quantities become later lane-load responses. |
| 20–21 | Actual-admission rule and total balance 4 | Changed loads, demand, sending and room change later actual lane entry. |
| 22 | Front-entry endpoint in 11 | Entry changes become readiness changes and feed later LTM limits 2–3. |
| 23 | Population-time objective 13, rewritten by conservation | Cumulative sink-exit responses become complete TST sensitivity. |

## Comparison purpose and evidence limits

V0–V3 isolate and combine traffic mechanisms; V4 reserves demand, turn-share, bay-storage/length, order and receiving combinations for robustness outside calibration/tuning. The order-only and storage-only cases are separate controls, not a strictly nested information ladder. The printed comparison models use public descriptive names rather than internal codes. The detailed baseline policy and original-series aggregate-inference allowance remain unchanged.

The movement-queue and lane/storage sources remain working model directions, not fully qualified current adapters. Their literature role is now connected to what the ablations should explain. The earlier exclusive-target mixture ablation remains unselected; this revision does not introduce a weaker comparator or select new baseline algorithms.

Feedback-13 exact arithmetic checks concern the separated admission and weighted-service balances, all three duration branches, cancellation for a fully cleared fixed head, and the optional shared-lane derivative. These are rational first-order identity checks, not executed traffic or numerical-gradient experiments. Existing reference checks remain historical evidence at their original scope. No simulator, AD/finite-difference traffic experiment, optimization, richer-reference run, package installation or numerical scenario calibration is undertaken. A complete, readable derivative path does not establish implementation correctness or external usefulness.

